Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: adapted from the story by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe

=
SHORT FILM ADAPTATION
=

FADE IN:

INT. HOUSE INTERIOR - NIGHT

A solitary figure, the NARRATOR, faces us in dim light. The old house holds its breath.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

He listens—to nothing we can yet hear.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

An OLD MAN asleep in bed. Shutters close fastened. Pitch dark.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye!

A pale blue eye, filmed—brief, cold.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT (MONTAGE)

Midnight. The Narrator turns the latch—oh, so gently—opens the door a head's width. A dark lantern, closed. His head enters with painful slowness.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

A single thin ray. The eye is closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - DAY

Morning. The Narrator enters boldly, hearty, kind.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And every morning, when the day broke, I went boldly into the chamber, and spoke courageously to him, calling him by name in a hearty tone, and inquiring how he has passed the night.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT (EIGHTH)

The door moves slower than a watch's minute hand. Darkness absolute.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity.

He chuckles under his breath. The Old Man moves on the bed, startled.

The Narrator does not draw back. Head in. About to open the lantern—

His thumb slips on the tin fastening.

OLD MAN
Who's there?

The Narrator freezes. Stillness. A long time. The Old Man remains sitting up, listening.

A slight groan—mortal terror.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew the sound well. I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart.

Patient waiting. Then—a very little crevice in the lantern.

A single dim ray, like the thread of a spider, falls full upon the vulture eye.

It is open—wide.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones.

A low, dull, quick sound—such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The beat quickens, louder. The Narrator holds the ray steady, scarcely breathing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The old man's hour had come!

With a loud yell he throws open the lantern and leaps. The Old Man shrieks once—once only.

The Narrator drags him to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him.

Silence strained by a muffled heart. Then—cessation.

The Narrator removes the bed. Hand upon the heart. Many minutes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Yes, he was stone, stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT (LATER)

Hasty silent labor. Dismemberment suggested in shadow—not lingered.

Three planks up from the flooring. The remains deposited between the scantlings. Boards replaced cunningly.

A tub. No stain left on the floor.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had been too wary for that. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

A bell sounds the hour—four. Still dark.

KNOCKING at the street door.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - PREDAWN

Three OFFICERS enter the chamber after a tour of the house. Suave. Searching.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A shriek had been heard by a neighbour during the night; suspicion of foul play had been aroused. I smiled,—for what had I to fear? The shriek, I said, was my own in a dream. The old man, I mentioned, was absent in the country.

Treasures shown—secure, undisturbed.

Chairs brought in. The Officers sit. The Narrator places his own seat upon the very spot beneath which the corpse reposes.

They chat of familiar things. He answers cheerily—then pales.

A ringing in his ears becomes distinct—then a low, dull, quick sound. Watch-in-cotton again.

He talks more freely, more fluently, higher, faster. The Officers do not react to the sound.

He rises, argues about trifles with violent gesticulations. Paces heavy. Grates his chair upon the boards.

The sound grows louder—louder—louder. The men chat on and smile.

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

HOLD on the floorboards. The beat dominates.

FADE OUT.

END.
